<p class="prose" style="font-size:13px; color:var(--text-tertiary); margin-bottom:32px;">Source for the four index figures: <a href="https://datalab.wto.org/Strait-of-Hormuz-Trade-Tracker">WTO-AXSMarine Strait of Hormuz Trade Tracker</a>.</p>

<div class="section-eyebrow">The framing</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Zero means different things for different commodities.</h2>
<p class="prose">The WTO-AXSMarine Strait of Hormuz Trade Tracker's three export series, crude oil, LNG, and fertilizer-related products, have all converged on the same figure this month: a 7-day moving average of zero. This is the flattest export record since the closure began. But zero transits through the strait does not mean the same thing for all three commodities. Crude, LNG, and fertilizer have each adapted in different ways, and the implications for dependent importers are different.</p>

<div class="section-eyebrow">What the export data shows</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">The grind to zero took six months. September is when it finished.</h2>
<p class="prose">All three series collapsed hard within the first four to six weeks of the closure, from a pre-closure baseline near 100 (each indexed to its own January-March 2025 average) into the single digits by April. In spite of regular announcements of memoranda of understanding and near-deals, none of the three ever recovered. Each kept grinding down through the summer (fertilizer: 7.5 in June, 6.6 in July, 2.0 in August; crude: 3.0 in July, 1.4 in August; LNG down to 0.9 in August), and September is the first month that grind has bottomed out completely. Fertilizer's zero run is unbroken since 23 August. Crude's started on 26 August. LNG's started earliest, 14 August.</p>
<p class="prose">Read as one number, this looks like total, undifferentiated collapse. But there are two other factors at play: whether a physical alternative exists for the commodity, and what the tracker is actually measuring.</p>

<div class="section-eyebrow">The tracker measures strait transits, not exports</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">A barrel that never enters the strait was never counted to begin with.</h2>
<p class="prose">The WTO-AXSMarine tracker is built on AIS vessel-tracking data inside the strait. A barrel that never enters the strait does not register as a missed export. It is never counted as strait traffic to begin with — a limitation we've written about at more length in <a href="/analysis/hormuz">Hormuz and the Limits of the Index</a>.</p>
<p class="prose">Saudi Arabia's East-West Pipeline, known as Petroline, runs 745 miles from the Eastern Province to Yanbu on the Red Sea, with a maximum capacity of 7 million barrels a day. Since the closure, Saudi Arabia has pushed the pipeline close to that ceiling. Roughly 5 million barrels a day of crude now moves out through Yanbu, plus 700,000 to 900,000 barrels a day of refined products. About 2 million barrels a day more feed domestic refineries sited along the pipeline's western terminus, chiefly at Yanbu and Rabigh; those refineries draw from the same pipeline flow. The result is that Saudi Arabia is reportedly still reaching roughly 85% of its pre-war export volume in normal conditions, almost entirely rerouted around the strait rather than through it. That figure held through most of the seven months, but it is not a fixed number, as September showed.</p>
<p class="prose">The UAE has a smaller but real version of the same option: the Habshan-Fujairah pipeline to the Gulf of Oman coast currently carries around 1.8 million barrels a day, with an expansion underway that would roughly double it to 3.6 million barrels a day, against pre-war UAE exports of about 3.4 million barrels a day. That expansion is targeted for operational status in 2027.</p>

<figure class="chart-figure">
  <img src="/images/hormuz-seven-months-sites-map.png" alt="Hormuz and its workarounds: the sites behind this piece" loading="lazy">
  <div class="chart-caption"><strong>Hormuz and its workarounds:</strong> the sites behind this piece. Schematic, not navigational — pipeline routes are illustrative paths between real endpoints, and the shaded chokepoint zones are approximate, not published JWC/war-risk boundaries.</div>
</figure>

<p class="prose">Why is not all crude routed this way as a matter of course, closure or not? Pipeline capacity is the binding constraint: Petroline's 7 million barrel ceiling and the Fujairah line's current 1.8 million fall well short of full Gulf export volume even combined, so the pipelines cannot carry everything regardless of preference. Loading directly at a Gulf terminal is also normally the shorter, cheaper voyage for cargo bound east, so producers use the pipeline mainly for cargo genuinely bound west or as a hedge. What has changed since February is the price of the alternative: VLCC freight to China has reportedly reached roughly $30 a barrel, close to a quarter of the total delivered cost of the barrel, which is what has pushed Saudi Arabia to run Petroline near its physical ceiling. We go deeper on where the rerouted barrels actually end up, and who it does and doesn't reach, in <a href="/analysis/hormuz-rebalancing">The Rebalancing Is Real. It Does Not Reach Everyone.</a></p>
<p class="prose">A meaningful share of the zero in the crude series is probably attributable to alternative routing. Barrels that used to transit Hormuz are now leaving from Yanbu and Fujairah instead, invisible to a strait-transit tracker. That routing is not itself free of risk: drone strikes hit Petroline itself on 10 and 11 September. Saudi Arabia shut the pipeline as a precaution, taking an estimated 4 to 5 million barrels a day of bypass capacity offline. Within a day, Houthi forces seized Mayyun (Perim) Island, challenging the security of the Bab-el-Mandeb strait, the same corridor that Yanbu-loaded cargo depends on to reach the Red Sea and beyond. For two and a half weeks, the strait and its main bypass route were both compromised at once. Petroline came back on line around 28 September at roughly 3.5 million barrels a day. Diverting around Hormuz does not mean diverting into a risk-free corridor, or even a fixed one. It means trading one designated risk for a different one.</p>
<figure class="chart-figure">
  <img src="/images/hormuz-seven-months-bab-el-mandeb.jpg" alt="Satellite view of the Bab-el-Mandeb strait and Mayyun (Perim) Island, Red Sea">
  <div class="chart-caption"><strong>Bab-el-Mandeb Strait, Red Sea.</strong> Mayyun (Perim) Island sits in the middle of the narrows, the position Houthi forces seized on 12 September. Photo credit: Coordenação-Geral de Observação da Terra/INPE.</div>
</figure>
<p class="prose">The price data backs up the routing explanation, and adds a wrinkle. Brent prices jumped on the initial closure, as expected, but after that first month the two series decouple entirely: Brent prices swooped from a roughly 118 dollar April and May peak down to about 72 in July and back to around 108 by September, while the export index stayed pinned near zero the whole time. Brent is a global benchmark blending many crude streams, so this is not a contradiction, but it does mean price action after the first month cannot be read as a live signal of Hormuz-specific supply. The market has not spent seven months pricing a full physical stoppage of Gulf crude, which is consistent with the pipeline-bypass and dark-transit picture above.</p>
<p class="prose">The two big swings in the second half of the window both trace to identifiable events, not to Hormuz-specific supply news. The July trough coincides with OPEC+ agreeing a fifth consecutive monthly output increase alongside both OPEC and the IEA cutting their 2026 demand growth forecasts and a large US crude inventory build in early August. The September climb is different: it lines up with the Petroline attack and the Mayyun seizure described above, the closest this window comes to the market visibly pricing a Hormuz-adjacent supply risk, briefly, before easing back as Petroline resumed flowing.</p>

