<div class="section-eyebrow">LNG and fertilizer do not have that option</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Pipeline bypass only works for a liquid that can move overland.</h2>
<p class="prose">Pipeline bypass works for crude because crude is a liquid that can move overland. It does not work for LNG or for solid bulk fertilizer.</p>
<p class="prose">LNG must be liquefied at the export terminal and moved by purpose-built LNG carriers. There is no overland pipeline substitute for liquefied gas at export scale. Qatar is the dominant supplier behind most Gulf LNG volumes and it lacks a meaningful alternative export route: no pipeline bypass exists at anything near LNG export scale, and building one would require years and heavy capital. Two Ras Laffan liquefaction trains were hit in a direct attack earlier this year, removing about 17% of Qatar's total export capacity, with repairs reportedly requiring three to five years. Qatar's undamaged capacity remains around 64 million tonnes a year. The constraint is export: cargo has queued and been turned back at the strait, more than 25 energy companies have declared force majeure since the war began, and Qatari LNG exports are not expected to return to pre-war normal even once Hormuz reopens.</p>
<figure class="chart-figure">
  <img src="/images/hormuz-seven-months-ras-laffan.jpg" alt="LNG carrier loading at Ras Laffan LNG Terminal, Qatar, with flare stacks in the foreground">
  <div class="chart-caption"><strong>Ras Laffan LNG Terminal, Qatar.</strong> Two of the terminal's liquefaction trains were hit in the direct attack described above, cutting roughly 17% of Qatar's export capacity. Photo credit: Nabeel Hashmi.</div>
</figure>
<p class="prose">Fertilizer is a solid bulk commodity with the same overland problem: urea, ammonia, and sulphur move by bulk carrier, and there is no pipeline equivalent for solids. Unlike LNG, fertilizer's shortfall appears to be a mix of two things. Reporting indicates Gulf urea production itself has been disrupted, with one estimate suggesting more than half of the Middle East's urea output has been lost since the war began, attributed in part to strikes on energy and industrial infrastructure that feed fertilizer plants. Separately, other reporting describes real, if unquantified, volumes of already-produced fertilizer stranded in the Gulf awaiting available ships.</p>

<div class="section-eyebrow">Squaring this against CDM's own Hormuz dependency data</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Zero on the tracker; not zero for whoever is on the other end of the trade.</h2>
<p class="prose">The Narrows <a href="/model">Chokepoint Dependency Model</a> ranks real 2024 bilateral trade exposure by importer for each commodity that transits Hormuz. Read against the export collapse, it reveals who is actually exposed — the question <a href="/analysis/hormuz-who-pays">Iran Can Hold Out. Here Is Who Pays.</a> asks at the country level across the whole crisis; this is the commodity-by-commodity version of the same question.</p>

