<p class="table-note">LNG (HS 271111). These dependency percentages describe marine-borne imports specifically, not total energy supply from all sources. Pakistan has no meaningful land-based gas alternative currently in service: the long-discussed Iran-Pakistan pipeline remains stalled over sanctions risk, and the rival Turkmenistan-Afghanistan-Pakistan-India line remains unbuilt. Given LNG's lack of a pipeline-bypass option, Pakistan appears to be carrying that exposure with no structural alternative visible in this data.</p>

<p class="prose"><strong>Fertilizer.</strong> India (48.4%), Thailand (53.0%), and Sri Lanka (65.8%) are the most exposed importers. The Narrows Chokepoint Dependency Model shows that none of the three have a viable alternative supplier, unlike Brazil, the US, and Mexico, which the model flags as having substitution options with a 30-45 day lag. Two of the world's other major fertilizer exporters have pulled back this year specifically because of this crisis. China has restricted roughly 50-80% of its phosphate export volume since March, citing the need to protect domestic supply after Hormuz disrupted global trade, and Russia is separately limiting nitrogen and phosphate volumes through its own quota system. For India, Thailand, and Sri Lanka, two conditions are hitting at once: the corridor they depend on most is at zero, and the backstop suppliers the rest of the market leans on are themselves constrained, for the same underlying reason. China's restraint has not been uniform across nutrients, though: phosphate stayed restricted, but China reversed course on nitrogen, reopening urea exports in late May after having held them back since March.</p>

