<div class="section-eyebrow">What this means for breadbasket regions</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">This is a food-security question, not only a shipping one.</h2>
<p class="prose">This is where the fertilizer collapse turns into a food-security question rather than a shipping one, and the exposure runs through major grain and protein producers themselves, not only through the countries most commonly discussed as food-insecure.</p>
<p class="prose">The United States imports 95% of the potash it uses, though the bulk of that, 79%, comes from Canada largely by rail rather than by sea, with Russia (12%) and Israel (3%) making up most of the rest and arriving by ship. Nitrogen and phosphate are far less import-reliant for the US, at 13% and 16% respectively. Brazil is a different case entirely: imports covered 88% of Brazil's total fertilizer consumption in 2025, with potash at 96%, nitrogen at 95%, and phosphate at 72%, and unlike the US case, Brazil's supply (from Canada, Russia, Belarus, and Morocco among others) arrives overwhelmingly by sea. One estimate puts the Strait of Hormuz's normal share of global fertilizer shipments at up to 30%, and roughly a third of global fertilizer trade as disrupted by this conflict in some form. Brazil is a top global exporter of soybeans and corn, so a fertilizer-cost shock reaching Brazilian farmers has food-price implications that extend well beyond Brazil's borders.</p>
<p class="prose">Reach continues beyond grain producers into other import-dependent regions. Sudan's Gulf-sourced fertilizer dependence is around 54%, the highest single-country exposure found in this research, and Bangladesh at 53.3%, applying the highest nitrogen-per-hectare usage rate among the countries covered, meaning it has the least margin to simply use less. The exposure is not limited to staple grains either: fertilizer-dependent cash crops in import-reliant growing regions carry the same input-cost risk, as already noted in Narrows' earlier <a href="/analysis/hormuz-dry-cargo">Hormuz Has a Fertilizer Problem Too</a>, which cited reporting on Cote d'Ivoire cocoa farmers going into the season short of fertilizer.</p>
<p class="prose">Urea, ammonia, and phosphate feed directly into the cost of the next planting season, and a fertilizer shock shows up with a lag, at the point farmers are buying inputs for the next crop. Given fertilizer's flatline has now run 5.5 weeks with no substitution option for the most exposed importers, and the usual secondary suppliers are simultaneously tightening for a related reason, the input-cost pressure on India, Thailand, Sri Lanka, Brazil, Sudan, and Bangladesh's next planting cycles looks less like a temporary disruption to absorb and more like a supply picture with no clear near-term relief valve.</p>

<p class="prose">On pricing itself: a futures and spot market exists for this, unlike for most of the corridor-level metrics elsewhere in this piece. CME/CBOT lists Urea (Granular) FOB US Gulf futures (ticker UFV) and a separate FOB Middle East contract (UME). The chart below plots the FOB Middle East contract against the fertilizer export index over the same window. Price rises through March and April as exports collapse, in line with what the export data alone would predict, but then falls for two straight months, back near its pre-closure level by late July, while exports stay pinned near zero the entire time. Price and physical export volume decouple well before the export data's own window ends, the same pattern as the crude chart above, and the urea data does not extend past 30 July, three weeks before the export index's final run to zero began, so it cannot say whether that decoupling held once the stoppage became total.</p>

<p class="prose">The decline itself is a China supply story, not a Hormuz story, and it is worth being precise about which China. China's phosphate restrictions, noted above, tightened supply and pushed the other direction. Urea moved the opposite way. Beijing had throttled urea exports since March as well, but reversed that stance on 26 May, setting export price floors of $660 a tonne FOB for prilled urea and $670 for granular, and allocating 1.5 to 1.6 million tonnes for June through August. The market felt it almost immediately: when India's National Fertilisers Limited floated a 1.7 million tonne tender on 10 June, it drew roughly 6.24 million tonnes in competing offers from about 34 suppliers, and the lowest bids came in around $445 to 449 a tonne, versus the $935 to 959 a tonne India had paid in an emergency April purchase at the height of the crisis. A seasonal lull after the Northern Hemisphere spring application window, plus Brazil pulling back on purchases because its own planting cycle had already concluded, added to an already oversupplied market. None of this reflects an easing of the Hormuz-specific squeeze on the corridor itself, which the export index shows staying at zero. It reflects a different, non-Gulf supplier stepping into the gap that China's own earlier restriction had helped create.</p>

