<div class="section-eyebrow">Seven months on</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Zero is not one story.</h2>
<p class="prose">For crude, it is largely a measurement artifact: the barrels are still moving, just around the tracker rather than through it, at a cost and a risk that briefly became very real when Petroline itself came under attack in September. For LNG, zero is close to the literal truth, a genuine capacity loss with no bypass and no fast repair. For fertilizer, zero sits on top of a market that is still finding its own price level independent of the closure, pulled down through the summer by a Chinese nitrogen-export reversal that had nothing to do with Hormuz, even as China's phosphate restrictions pulled the other way.</p>
<p class="prose">What ties the three together is not the export index. It is what each zero forces someone else to absorb. Japan and South Korea carry crude exposure with the least practical pipeline relief per barrel. Pakistan carries LNG exposure with no structural alternative at all. India, Thailand, Sri Lanka, Sudan, and Bangladesh carry fertilizer exposure into a planting season where the usual backstop suppliers are themselves constrained, for reasons that trace back to the same closure. Seven months in, the strait's most durable effect may not be the zero on the tracker, but the fact that the costs of avoiding that zero, in pipeline capacity, in freight, in a second chokepoint's own war risk, have quietly been reassigned to whoever had the least room to absorb them.</p>

